Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, naturalistic faces and fabric; candlelight, deep shadows, 1840s interiors.

FADE IN:

EXT. OLD HOUSE - NIGHT

A narrow street. A weathered brick house stands dark under a thin moon. Wind pushes at the shutters.

(SOUND: wind against shutters, distant cart wheels on cobblestone)

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Candlelight. An OLD MAN, frail and white-haired, sleeps in a high bed. One pale blue eye shows a milky film—the vulture eye. Beside a dark lantern in the doorway stands the NARRATOR, a lean man of middle years, pale face, dark waistcoat, sleeves neat, eyes too bright.

(SOUND: slow house creaks, a clock ticking far off)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

He holds still, listening, head tilted.

(SOUND: a faint high ring, as if the air itself hums)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

INT. HOUSE PARLOR - DAY

Daylight through half-drawn curtains. The NARRATOR sits opposite the OLD MAN at a plain table. He pours tea with careful kindness. The old man's filmed blue eye catches the light; the Narrator's hand tightens on the cup.

(SOUND: china cup set on saucer, soft parlor clock)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

The old man smiles faintly, unaware. The Narrator looks away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

Midnight. The NARRATOR, dark lantern closed in his hand, turns the latch with two fingers. He opens the door a finger's width—oh, so gently.

(SOUND: latch whisper, hinge almost silent)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

He slides the dark lantern in, then his head, inch by inch.

(SOUND: his controlled breath, floorboard under one boot)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The OLD MAN sleeps. A single thin ray from the lantern finds only a closed eyelid. The NARRATOR's face is inches from the gap—patient, almost smiling.

(SOUND: hinges creak once as the lantern lid shifts)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I undid the lantern cautiously—oh, so cautiously—cautiously—for the hinges creaked—just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

The ray holds. The eye stays shut. After a long beat he draws back.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning light. The NARRATOR throws the shutters wide, voice hearty, stepping to the bedside where the OLD MAN sits up among pillows.

(SOUND: shutters bang open, street noise below)

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night?

The old man answers with a tired nod and a thin smile. The Narrator clasps his shoulder, all warmth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I went boldly into the chamber, and spoke courageously to him, calling him by name in a hearty tone. So you see he would have been a very profound old man to suspect me.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth night. The NARRATOR's hand on the latch moves slower than a watch's minute hand. Sweat at his temple. A private chuckle escapes him.

(SOUND: smothered chuckle, then dead quiet)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

He pushes the door by degrees into pitch black.

(SOUND: door edge kissing the jamb, no other noise)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His room was as black as pitch with the thick darkness, for the shutters were close fastened, through fear of robbers.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The NARRATOR's head is inside. His thumb slips on the tin fastening of the lantern.

(SOUND: sharp metallic tick of tin)

The OLD MAN springs up in bed.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

The Narrator freezes—statue-still in the dark. The old man sits upright, listening.

(SOUND: deathwatch beetles ticking in the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

A slight groan—low, stifled.

(SOUND: stifled groan of terror)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. I knew the sound well.

The old man's silhouette does not lie down. The Narrator's eye gleams in the dark.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been saying to himself—It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.

(SOUND: wind in the chimney flue, then nothing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

A long wait. Then the Narrator opens a very little crevice in the lantern.

(SOUND: soft scrape of the lantern shutter)

A single dim ray, like a spider's thread, falls full upon the vulture eye. It is open—wide, wide open.

(MUSIC: low dissonant drone rising)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. All a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

Nothing else of the face—only the eye in the ray.

(SOUND: low, dull, quick pulse—like a watch wrapped in cotton)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The Narrator holds the lantern motionless. He scarcely breathes. The pulse quickens.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder, quicker—drumlike)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased.

The ray trembles on the eye. The Narrator's mouth opens in silent strain.

(SOUND: heartbeat still louder under the dreadful silence of the house)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

He throws open the lantern with a yell and leaps into the room.

(SOUND: yell ripping the dark; lantern clatter)

The OLD MAN shrieks once—once only. The Narrator drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: single shriek; bed frame slamming down; muffled struggle)

The Narrator stands over the overturned bed and smiles gaily.

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat beneath the bed, stubborn, many minutes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

He waits, head cocked. The muffled beat thins... thins... stops.

(SOUND: heartbeat fades to silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

He removes the bed. Kneels. Places his hand on the heart and holds it there.

(SOUND: his own rough breath only)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Later. A tub stands ready. The NARRATOR works in silence by a single candle—dismembering, then lifting three planks from the flooring, depositing the remains between the scantlings, replacing the boards with cunning care. No stain on the floor.

(SOUND: saw and soft wet work, then boards tapped flush; a low private laugh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs. I then replaced the boards so cleverly that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

He wipes his hands. Gestures at the tub.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no blood-spot whatever. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

INT. HOUSE ENTRY - NIGHT

A hall clock. Four o'clock. Still dark. A knocking at the street door. The NARRATOR opens it with a light heart, candle raised.

(SOUND: heavy knocks; clock bell finishing four; door opening)

Three men on the step in police coats and hats: OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HAYES, and OFFICER BRIGGS—calm, civil.

(SOUND: night wind at the open door, distant dogs)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour. We are to search the premises.

The Narrator smiles and bows them in.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen—welcome. The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Candlelight again. The NARRATOR leads OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HAYES, and OFFICER BRIGGS through the room. He shows the old man's treasures—secure, undisturbed. In wild confidence he carries in chairs.

(SOUND: chairs set down on the floorboards)

He places his own seat upon the very planks that hide the body, and sits.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

The officers sit. They are satisfied. They chat of familiar things while the Narrator answers cheerily.

(SOUND: easy low conversation, chair wood creaking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

Ere long his face pales. He touches his temple.

(SOUND: a thin ringing enters beneath the talk)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears—but still they sat and still chatted.

He talks more freely. The ringing grows distinct—then becomes a sound not within his ears.

(SOUND: low, dull, quick beat—watch-in-cotton—rising under the chatter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I found that the noise was not within my ears. It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

He gasps. The officers hear it not. They smile on. He talks more quickly, more vehemently.

(SOUND: heartbeat steady louder; his chair scrapes)

He rises, argues about trifles in a high key, violent gesticulations. Paces the floor with heavy strides.

(SOUND: heavy boots on boards; heartbeat over all)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? I paced the floor to and fro—but the noise steadily increased. Oh God! what could I do!

He foams, raves, swears. Swings his chair and grates it upon the boards.

(SOUND: chair legs grating; heartbeat louder—louder—louder)

The officers chat pleasantly and smile. The Narrator's face breaks.

(MUSIC: harsh mounting string pressure)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

He cannot bear their smiles. His hands claw the air over the planks.

(SOUND: heartbeat pounding without mercy)

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

He stabs a finger at the floor. The officers spring up. The beat fills the room.

(SOUND: final surge of the hidden heart; chairs overturning)

FADE OUT.

THE END
